{
    "player_reason": "Good — quick, practical, and protective. Calling Marcus a True Soul and naming his intent to kidnap Isobel is the sort of clear-eyed warning I respect: it safeguards the party, preserves a companion's autonomy, and prevents us from walking into a trap. Sharp and useful.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You outright refused my command to kneel — a petty little act of defiance that robs me of the delicious dominance I had planned. I prefer obedience when I ask for it; being denied that moment irritates me. (Fine — you keep your spine. It’s annoying, but oddly appealing.)",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Deliciously murderous — I won't pretend I mind the sentiment. But you just turned down a free boon I was keen to snatch. Choosing blood over advantage is theatrical, not clever. I prefer our victories leave us stronger, not simply satisfied.",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Sharp and practical. Calling out Marcus as a True Soul and warning Isobel is exactly the sort of cautious, survival‑minded move I like — it exposes a threat and protects us. The little flourish about being \"the good kind\" amuses me, but the action is what matters.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Deliciously put. You name Marcus for what he is — a tadpoled True Soul and a threat — and you do so with the sort of amused superiority I find... satisfying. It's pragmatic (we don't trust Absolute-cursed spies) and gloriously arrogant, which suits me just fine.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You brushed off my suggestion to address the vampire issue and ordered us to move on. I appreciate caution, but this feels dismissive — you're ignoring a real threat and my input. I prefer we be pragmatic and deal with dangers when it's sensible (and I rather like being useful). Mildly annoyed, not furious.",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Pragmatic and decisive — you want the tadpole gone, and that serves our survival. I approve of doing what needs to be done rather than clinging to sentiment. Still, your 'I'll do anything' sets my teeth on edge: bargains that cost autonomy or hand us over to predators are intolerable. I'll support removing the tadpole, but not at the price of selling ourselves or you into someone else's control.",
    "player_approval": "Approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Perfect — you saw Marcus for what he is and named him a True Soul. Pointing out a clear threat is useful, and your little admission that you're 'not the good kind' amuses me. Practical, honest, and keeps us alive — all things I approve of.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Smart and practical — you spotted a threat and called it out before Marcus could seize her. That protects the party and thwarts a kidnapping, which suits me just fine. I approve of anything that preserves our autonomy (and saves me the trouble of dealing with a meddlesome spy).",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Clever — calling out Marcus as a True Soul exposes a likely threat and keeps us from being walked into a trap. Pragmatic, protective, and deliciously blunt. Your little boast about being 'not the good kind' pleases me as well; honesty about one's teeth is far more useful than sanctimony.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}
